     "\[Rule]", "\<\"Davide Fioravanti and Marco Rossi\"\>"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"title\"\>", 
     "\[Rule]", "\<\"On the origin of the correspondence between classical \
and quantum\\n  integrable theories\"\>"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"comments\"\>", 
     "\[Rule]", "\<\"More comprehensive and explicit version (v2 is not to be \
considered\\n  as appeared wrongly preceded by v1)\"\>"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"journal-ref\"\>", "\[Rule]", "Null"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"doi\"\>", 
     "\[Rule]", "\<\"10.1016/j.physletb.2023.137706\"\>"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"report-no\"\>", "\[Rule]", "Null"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"categories\"\>", 
     "\[Rule]", "\<\"hep-th math-ph math.MP nlin.SI\"\>"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"license\"\>", 
     "\[Rule]", \
"\<\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\"\>"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"abstract\"\>", 
     "\[Rule]", "\<\"  If we start from certain functional relations as \
definition of a quantum\\nintegrable theory, then we can derive from them a \
linear integral equation. It\\ncan be extended, by introducing dynamical \
variables, to become an equation with\\nthe form of Marchenko's. Then, we \
derive from the latter a classical\\n(differential) Lax pair. We exemplify \
our method by focusing on the massive\\nversion of the ODE/IM (Ordinary \
Differential Equations/Integrable Models)\\ncorrespondence from Quantum \
sine-Gordon (sG) with many moduli/masses to the\\nclassical sinh-Gordon (shG) \
equation, so describing, in a particular case, some\\nsuper-symmetric gauge \
theories and the $AdS_3$ strong coupling scattering\\namplitudes/Wilson \
loops. Yet, we present it in a way which reveals its\\ngenerality of \
application. In fact, we give some hints on how it works for \
spin\\nchains.\\n\"\>"}], ",", 
